<body><section class='page'><h1>CareConnect NGO</h1><p>Project Documentation and System Analysis Report</p><p>A browser-based donation, volunteering, impact communication, and private supporter activity prototype</p><p>Prepared for academic submission</p><p>Project repository: CareConnectNGO</p><p>Report date: 27 October 2026</p><p>Prepared from the implemented files index.html, donation.html, login.html, styles.css, script.js, donation.js, auth.js, chatbot.js, and the GitHub Pages deployment workflow.</p><div class='cover-mark'>CARE<br/>CONNECT</div><div class='cover-note'>Every child deserves a chance to shine.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Certificate</h1><p>This is to certify that the project report entitled CareConnect NGO: Project Documentation and System Analysis Report has been prepared as an academic documentation of the CareConnectNGO web prototype.</p><p>The report describes the implemented user interface, client-side interaction flows, browser storage behavior, validation rules, privacy messaging, and deployment arrangement visible in the project repository.</p><p>The implementation is a static front-end demonstration. References to a production database, payment gateway, staff portal, or server-side security are presented as recommended extensions and are not represented as delivered functionality.</p><p>The report is suitable for review as a project design, implementation, and validation document.</p><table class='signature'><tr><td>Project Guide<br/><br/>________________________</td><td>Student<br/><br/>________________________</td></tr><tr><td colspan='2'>Date: ________________________</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Declaration</h1><p>I declare that this report is a faithful technical description of the CareConnectNGO prototype available in the project workspace at the time of writing.</p><p>The report distinguishes implemented behavior from proposed production architecture. The demo uses localStorage for browser-local activity and profile data, demonstrates payment instructions and a QR-code request, and does not process a real payment.</p><p>Any sample names, statistics, donor entries, images, currencies, and impact figures displayed by the web pages are treated as synthetic demonstration data unless independently verified by the project owner.</p><p>The purpose of this document is to explain the system, its design decisions, its current limits, and the next engineering steps required for a safe and scalable NGO platform.</p><div class='declaration-box'>Declaration status: Documentation of implemented prototype<br/>Data classification: Synthetic demo data<br/>Production payment processing: Not implemented</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>1 Abstract</h1><p>CareConnect NGO is a warm, public-facing web prototype designed to connect supporters with children&#226;€™s education and wellbeing initiatives. Its primary experience is a single-page public portal containing mission messaging, impact figures, program cards, donation needs, campaign progress, a volunteer form, a supporter story, and a lightweight help chatbot.</p><p>A dedicated donation page extends the public experience with contribution types for money, food, clothes, and learning spaces. It supports currency selection, preset and custom amounts, anonymous giving, donor-list visibility, private activity lookup, generated payment instructions, and a QR-code request. A separate login page creates a browser-local supporter profile for viewing activity.</p><p>The implementation deliberately demonstrates front-end interaction without claiming to be a production financial system. HTML supplies semantic structure, CSS supplies the visual system and responsive layout, and JavaScript supplies form handling, local persistence, filtering, modals, profile rendering, and chatbot responses.</p><p>The report analyzes the system through motivation, requirements, stakeholder needs, architecture, data modeling, interface design, implementation, outputs, validation, limitations, and future scope. It recommends a three-tier production architecture with a secure API, relational database, hosted payment provider, role-based staff portal, audit logs, and privacy-by-design controls.</p><div class='abstract-highlight'><strong>Keywords:</strong> NGO portal, donation workflow, volunteer engagement, impact dashboard, browser storage, privacy, responsive UI, validation, static web prototype.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Table of Contents</h1><p>The following page plan uses a page break for each major report unit. Page numbers are stable in the generated Word-compatible document.</p><table><tr><th>Sr. No.</th><th>Title</th><th>Page No.</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Abstract</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Introduction</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>2.1â€</section>
<section class='page'><h1>List of Figures and Tables</h1><p>Figure 1. Public portal information architecture.</p><p>Figure 2. Context-level data flow diagram.</p><p>Figure 3. Conceptual entity relationship diagram.</p><p>Figure 4. Donation workflow and browser persistence.</p><p>Figure 5. Responsive interface regions.</p><p>Table 1. Stakeholder and responsibility matrix.</p><p>Table 2. Functional requirement catalogue.</p><p>Table 3. Validation and test matrix.</p><p>Table 4. Implemented versus proposed capability matrix.</p><table><tr><th>Artifact</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Report location</th></tr><tr><td>DFD</td><td>Shows actors, browser interface, local storage, and external QR service boundary</td><td>Page 25</td></tr><tr><td>ERD</td><td>Models donor, contribution, campaign, profile, and activity concepts</td><td>Page 27</td></tr><tr><td>Validation matrix</td><td>Connects inputs to browser validation and expected messages</td><td>Page 46</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>2 Introduction</h1><p>Non-governmental organizations depend on trust. A visitor must quickly understand the mission, a supporter must know how to help, and an organization must communicate outcomes without exposing sensitive people or financial details.</p><p>CareConnect addresses this communication problem through a focused web experience. It places empathy-led content beside practical calls to action and makes donation choices concrete through school supplies, meals, learning support, and spaces.</p><p>The present repository implements the public-facing prototype as static HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The browser loads pages directly, and the JavaScript files attach behavior after the document is available.</p><p>This chapter introduces the context of the project. Later chapters analyze the exact implementation and then separate current prototype behavior from the roadmap in the README for a full NGO management system.</p><div class='quote'>A useful NGO interface must make impact understandable, participation approachable, and privacy visible.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>2.1 Motivation</h1><p>Small NGOs often operate with limited staff and fragmented communication channels. A public website can reduce repeated questions, give supporters a clear entry point, and make the organization&#226;€™s work easier to understand.</p><p>The motivation for CareConnect is not only to collect donations. It is to show a relationship between a contribution and a human outcome: a notebook becomes a learning tool, a meal supports concentration, and a welcoming space creates continuity.</p><p>The interface uses visual warmth, short explanations, impact numbers, campaign progress, and a simple volunteer form to reduce cognitive effort. The design is intentionally less like an administrative dashboard and more like an invitation to join a community.</p><p>The prototype also explores privacy-aware supporter features. Anonymous giving is explicit, profile activity is described as private, and the interface warns that the implementation is a browser-local demo with no stored payment details.</p><ul><li>Improve public understanding of NGO work.</li><li>Offer multiple contribution paths.</li><li>Demonstrate transparent progress communication.</li><li>Make privacy controls understandable to non-technical visitors.</li></ul></section>
<section class='page'><h1>2.2 Problem Statement</h1><p>The problem is to design a trustworthy and accessible web portal that explains an NGO&#226;€™s education and wellbeing work, guides a visitor toward an appropriate action, records demo activity without exposing private information, and remains usable on different screen sizes.</p><p>Without a unified portal, donors may not know what is needed, volunteers may not know how to participate, and supporters may have no convenient way to revisit their own contributions. Public impact communication may also become disconnected from the actions that create it.</p><p>The prototype problem is intentionally bounded. It does not attempt to manage real children&#226;€™s records, real financial settlement, staff permissions, or a full operational ledger. It instead demonstrates the public experience and the browser-side interaction model that can later be connected to secure services.</p><p>A successful solution must balance emotional communication with explicit limitations. It must not imply that a generated QR code, localStorage entry, or demo login is equivalent to a regulated payment or identity system.</p><div class='requirement-box'><strong>Problem definition:</strong> Build an approachable and transparent public NGO web experience that supports giving, volunteering, impact communication, and private demo activity while preserving a clear boundary around production security.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>2.3 Purpose, Objectives and Goals</h1><p>The purpose of CareConnect is to demonstrate how a small NGO can present its mission and convert visitor interest into measurable actions: learning about programs, selecting a need, making a donation intention, volunteering, or asking a question.</p><p>The first objective is communication. The home page must explain why the organization exists, what it does, and what current support needs look like.</p><p>The second objective is participation. Donation options, campaign tabs, a volunteer form, and a chatbot should give users several low-friction ways to engage.</p><p>The third objective is trust. Privacy notes, anonymous giving, private activity language, accessible labels, visible validation, and an explicit demo-payment warning establish honest expectations.</p><p>The long-term goal is a complete NGO management system. The README proposes education programs, inventory, donations, expenses, volunteer coordination, aggregate dashboards, audit logs, and role-based staff access as later modules.</p><table><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Measure in the prototype</th></tr><tr><td>Explain mission</td><td>Hero, story, programs, quote, and footer content</td></tr><tr><td>Enable giving</td><td>Donation page with type, currency, amount, privacy, and payment modal</td></tr><tr><td>Support return visits</td><td>Browser-local profile and activity view</td></tr><tr><td>Answer common questions</td><td>Keyword-based helper chatbot</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>2.4 Literature Survey</h1><p>Digital fundraising research commonly emphasizes clarity of purpose, visible outcomes, low-friction forms, trust signals, and confirmation after an action. CareConnect reflects these principles through campaign progress bars, need-specific amounts, supporter stories, and a completion modal.</p><p>Human-centered design literature stresses that users should understand what a system is doing, receive immediate feedback, and recover from errors. The prototype uses aria-live message regions, browser-native required and email validation, selected states, modal close controls, and explanatory notes.</p><p>Privacy-by-design guidance recommends data minimization, purpose limitation, transparency, and user control. The current system stores a small donor activity object in localStorage and gives the user an anonymous option. This is useful for demonstration, but localStorage is not a suitable store for sensitive production records.</p><p>Secure payment guidance recommends redirecting payment details to a trusted provider rather than collecting card numbers in the application. The prototype follows the safer direction by showing demo bank and UPI instructions, but a production integration would need server-side verification, webhook handling, reconciliation, and fraud controls.</p><div class='citation-note'>The literature survey is a design synthesis. Formal sources and standards used to guide the recommendations are listed in Chapter 8.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>2.5 Project Scope</h1><p>In scope are the public home page, impact section, program cards, needs list, donation tracker, volunteer form, donation page, currency display, anonymous contribution option, supporter list, profile lookup, login demo, modal interactions, helper chatbot, responsive styling, and GitHub Pages deployment configuration.</p><p>The scope includes synthetic data and browser-local persistence. It includes a documented distinction between public supporter display and private activity, but it does not include a server, database, staff administration, real authentication, or a real payment processor.</p><p>The scope also includes a quality review of labels, required fields, email fields, minimum password length, modal semantics, keyboard escape handling, and responsive navigation.</p><p>Out-of-scope capabilities are treated as future work: real account recovery, multi-factor authentication, role management, volunteer approval, inventory transactions, expenses, financial reconciliation, child records, audit events, and verified impact reporting.</p><table><tr><th>Included</th><th>Excluded from current prototype</th></tr><tr><td>Public communication and demo giving flow</td><td>Real payment settlement</td></tr><tr><td>Local supporter activity</td><td>Centralized database</td></tr><tr><td>Responsive browser UI</td><td>Staff portal and RBAC</td></tr><tr><td>Static deployment</td><td>Server-side audit and reporting</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3 System Analysis</h1><p>System analysis converts the project idea into actors, capabilities, information flows, constraints, and observable outcomes. The analysis below is based on the source files in the repository and does not infer unavailable backend behavior.</p><p>At runtime, the browser loads HTML documents and linked assets. JavaScript selects elements by id or class, registers event listeners, validates or transforms inputs, updates the DOM, and writes selected objects to localStorage.</p><p>The public home page is a single document with anchored sections. The donation and login experiences are separate documents that reuse the same stylesheet and visual language.</p><p>The system is therefore best described as a client-side static web prototype with a browser persistence layer and one external image/QR dependency. It is not yet a multi-user web application.</p><div class='architecture-line'>Browser â†’ HTML pages â†’ CSS presentation + JavaScript behavior â†’ localStorage / external QR image request</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.1 Existing Systems</h1><p>The current implementation consists of three user-facing pages. index.html is the public landing and storytelling page. donation.html is the contribution and private activity page. login.html is the demo sign-in entry point.</p><p>The JavaScript surfaces are intentionally separated. script.js handles home-page navigation, the first donation modal, donation tracker tabs, volunteer submission feedback, and the homepage helper widget. donation.js handles currencies, donor rendering, profile setup, activity, payment details, and supporter modal behavior. auth.js handles the login form.</p><p>chatbot.js is shared by the home, donation, and login pages. styles.css is shared across all pages and contains the design tokens, layouts, components, modal styles, forms, and responsive rules.</p><p>The repository also includes a GitHub Actions workflow that uploads the repository root as a GitHub Pages artifact and deploys it when changes reach the main branch.</p><table><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Primary file</th><th>Role</th></tr><tr><td>Public portal</td><td>index.html</td><td>Mission, impact, programs, needs, volunteer form</td></tr><tr><td>Giving flow</td><td>donation.html</td><td>Contribution, profile, donor list, payment modal</td></tr><tr><td>Demo login</td><td>login.html</td><td>Browser-local identity entry</td></tr><tr><td>Deployment</td><td>.github/workflows/pages.yml</td><td>GitHub Pages publishing</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.2 Scope and Limitations of Existing Systems</h1><p>The implemented scope is effective for a portfolio demonstration because it can be opened as static files, requires no installation, and makes its primary interactions visible immediately.</p><p>The same simplicity creates limitations. localStorage is origin-specific and user-controlled, so it cannot provide reliable central records, access control, data retention, concurrent updates, backups, or auditability.</p><p>The demo login validates a name, email, and minimum password length but does not verify a password against a server. The password itself is not stored, which is positive for the demo, but the resulting profile is not an authenticated account.</p><p>The payment modal generates random demo account and UPI values in the browser and requests a QR image from an external service. No payment confirmation is verified. Public metrics and donor examples are static content rather than records calculated from a trusted ledger.</p><div class='warning'>Important limitation: the current site must not be used to collect real passwords, real payment details, or identifiable childrenâ€™s information.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.3 Project Perspective and Features</h1><p>The project perspective is a public trust and engagement layer for an education-focused NGO. It is designed to sit in front of operational services that would eventually manage programs, volunteers, inventory, donations, expenses, and impact records.</p><p>The implemented features are organized around the supporter journey: discover the mission, understand programs, see current needs, choose a contribution type, provide contact information, choose public or anonymous display, receive demo payment instructions, and revisit activity.</p><p>The visual system reinforces this journey through a dark green trust color, lime calls to action, cream paper backgrounds, serif headings, rounded cards, impact counters, and responsive layouts.</p><p>The feature set also includes graceful communication of uncertainty. The donation page says it is a demo, the login page says not to use a real password, and the activity text describes storage as browser-local.</p><ul><li>Mission and impact storytelling</li><li>Program and need discovery</li><li>Donation type and currency selection</li><li>Anonymous/public supporter choice</li><li>Private activity display</li><li>Volunteer contact form</li><li>FAQ-style helper chatbot</li><li>Responsive navigation and modals</li></ul></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.4 Stakeholders</h1><p>Stakeholders are people or groups affected by the system or responsible for its operation. The public website has several distinct stakeholder needs that should not be collapsed into one generic user profile.</p><p>Visitors need a fast, understandable introduction and a clear way to participate. Donors need confidence about purpose, visibility, privacy, and next steps. Volunteers need a low-friction invitation to start a conversation.</p><p>NGO communications staff need editable stories, programs, progress figures, and contact details. Finance staff need a trusted source of donation and expense records, which the current prototype does not provide. Program staff need education and attendance workflows, also reserved for future work.</p><p>Children and families are indirect but critical stakeholders. They benefit from the work but should not be exposed through public pages, sample APIs, or identifiable stories without consent and safeguarding review.</p><table><tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Need</th><th>Current response</th></tr><tr><td>Visitor</td><td>Understand mission quickly</td><td>Hero, story, programs, impact</td></tr><tr><td>Donor</td><td>Give with clarity and privacy</td><td>Donation form and anonymous option</td></tr><tr><td>Volunteer</td><td>Find an approachable entry point</td><td>Get involved form</td></tr><tr><td>NGO staff</td><td>Manage trusted records</td><td>Future backend requirement</td></tr><tr><td>Child/family</td><td>Safety and data minimization</td><td>No child records in prototype</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.5 Requirement Analysis: Functional Requirements</h1><p>Functional requirements describe observable capabilities. The home page shall provide anchored navigation for Home, About us, Our work, Impact, Stories, Donate, and Log in. The header shall also expose a Get involved action.</p><p>The system shall display impact counts, program cards, needs, campaign tracker cards, a story quote, and a volunteer form. It shall show a success message and reset the volunteer form after a valid submission.</p><p>The donation page shall allow a contribution category, supported currency, preset amount, optional custom amount, name, email, anonymous selection, and continuation to a payment-details modal. The page shall maintain a supporter list and render private activity for a matching email in the current browser.</p><p>The login page shall require name, valid email, and a password of at least six characters. On success it shall write a browser-local user object and redirect to the profile section. The chatbot shall answer keyword-matched questions about donations, currency, privacy, profiles, and volunteering.</p><table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Priority</th></tr><tr><td>FR-01</td><td>Present mission and impact content</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>FR-02</td><td>Provide donation and volunteer entry points</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>FR-03</td><td>Record demo activity locally</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>FR-04</td><td>Respect anonymous display choice</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>FR-05</td><td>Provide helper responses</td><td>Medium</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.5 Requirement Analysis: Performance Requirements</h1><p>The prototype should load as a static site without a build step. Its small source files and shared stylesheet support a fast first render on ordinary broadband and mobile connections.</p><p>Interactions should respond immediately after a click or form event. Selection states, tracker filtering, modal open/close, and localStorage rendering should complete synchronously for the small demo data set.</p><p>The system should remain usable at narrow viewport widths. The stylesheet includes a menu toggle and responsive layout rules so multi-column areas can collapse rather than force horizontal scrolling.</p><p>Production performance requirements are more demanding: compressed assets, cache headers, an image optimization strategy, a content delivery network, server-side pagination for donor and activity data, database indexes, monitored API latency, and graceful degradation when external services are unavailable.</p><table><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Prototype expectation</th><th>Production direction</th></tr><tr><td>Interaction response</td><td>Immediate local DOM update</td><td>API feedback within agreed SLA</td></tr><tr><td>Page payload</td><td>Small static files</td><td>Budgeted and monitored assets</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile layout</td><td>No forced desktop layout</td><td>Device and accessibility testing</td></tr><tr><td>External dependency</td><td>QR image request</td><td>Timeout, fallback, monitoring</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>3.5 Requirement Analysis: Security Requirements</h1><p>The prototype must clearly prevent users from mistaking browser-local demo behavior for secure authentication or payment processing. The login page explicitly says not to use a real password, and the donation page says no payment details are stored.</p><p>Production authentication must use a server, strong password hashing, secure sessions or short-lived tokens, rate limiting, account recovery, role-based authorization, and audit events. The browser must never be treated as the authority for identity or permissions.</p><p>Production donation handling must use a hosted payment provider or tokenized integration. Card numbers, CVVs, bank credentials, and secrets must not be stored in this front end. Payment webhooks must be signature-verified and reconciled with a trusted provider record.</p><p>Privacy controls must prevent publication of anonymous donor names, prevent public access to private activity, minimize personal data, and define retention and deletion policies. No children&#226;€™s personal data should be added to public pages or sample endpoints.</p><div class='security-grid'><div><strong>Current demo</strong><br/>Browser-local state<br/>Synthetic data<br/>No real payment</div><div><strong>Required production</strong><br/>Server authority<br/>Encrypted transport<br/>Verified payment and audit</div></div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4 System Design</h1><p>System design translates the requirements into a set of views, data flows, interface states, and boundaries. The design is intentionally modular even though the current implementation is a static site.</p><p>The public view is separated from the private supporter activity view. This is a conceptual separation in the current browser, not a server-enforced authorization boundary.</p><p>The donation workflow is designed as a sequence of selection, validation, local activity creation, supporter-list update, and payment-instruction display. The flow is suitable for a demo and provides a clear seam for a future payment API.</p><p>The recommended production design retains the public experience but moves identity, records, payment confirmation, and reporting into protected services.</p><div class='design-sequence'>Discover â†’ Choose need â†’ Validate details â†’ Record intention â†’ Show next step â†’ Review private activity</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.1 Design Constraints</h1><p>The first constraint is the static hosting model. GitHub Pages can serve files but cannot execute a private server, database transaction, payment webhook, or staff authorization policy.</p><p>The second constraint is the demo data model. The site uses arrays of donor and activity objects in localStorage, which is appropriate for a single browser but not for a shared organization-wide dataset.</p><p>The third constraint is trust and safeguarding. The report must not represent synthetic figures, sample names, or generated payment details as verified organizational records.</p><p>The fourth constraint is usability across desktop and mobile layouts. The design must preserve readable text, visible focus/selection states, actionable controls, and a navigation path when the viewport becomes narrow.</p><table><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Design response</th></tr><tr><td>Static hosting</td><td>Client-only behavior and explicit demo language</td></tr><tr><td>No server database</td><td>localStorage limited to synthetic browser activity</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy and safety</td><td>No child records, anonymous option, no real passwords</td></tr><tr><td>Responsive use</td><td>Shared CSS, menu toggle, flexible grids</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.2 System Model: Architecture</h1><p>The current architecture has four visible layers. The document layer contains semantic HTML pages and forms. The presentation layer is styles.css, which defines colors, typography, spacing, cards, buttons, modals, and responsive layouts.</p><p>The behavior layer contains page-specific JavaScript. script.js is associated with the home page, donation.js with the donation page, auth.js with login, and chatbot.js with the shared chat widget.</p><p>The persistence layer is the browser&#226;€™s localStorage. The keys careconnectUser, careconnectDonors, and careconnectActivity hold simple JSON values. This layer is local to the browser profile and can be cleared or modified by the user.</p><p>An external QR image service is requested by donation.js. In production, the boundary should instead be a trusted payment provider or a locally generated, verified payment artifact.</p><pre>Visitor<br/>  â†“<br/>HTML pages -- styles.css<br/>  â†“<br/>JavaScript modules<br/>  â†“<br/>localStorage + external QR image request<br/><br/>Future: secure API -- relational database -- payment provider</pre></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.2 System Model: Context-Level DFD</h1><p>The context-level data flow diagram treats the CareConnect site as one system. Visitors provide browsing input, contribution selections, contact details, and questions. The system returns content, validation messages, activity summaries, payment instructions, and chatbot responses.</p><p>The browser storage boundary contains only the demo profile, donor display entries, and activity records created in that browser. It is not an authoritative organization record.</p><p>The external QR image request receives an encoded demo payment URI. It is an integration boundary and should be treated as unavailable or untrusted until a production provider is selected and verified.</p><p>The future staff and finance actors are shown as planned system boundaries rather than implemented actors. Their records must be protected by server-side authentication and role authorization.</p><pre>+-------------+       browse / submit        +-----------------------+<br/>| Visitor     | ---------------------------> | CareConnect browser   |<br/>| donor       | <--------------------------- | interface             |<br/>+-------------+    content / feedback         +----------+------------+<br/>                                                         |<br/>                                      read/write demo   | request QR image<br/>                                                         v                  v<br/>                                             +----------------+   +----------------+<br/>                                             | localStorage   |   | QR image API   |<br/>                                             +----------------+   +----------------+</pre></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.2 System Model: DFD Data Flows</h1><p>Flow A is public discovery. The browser loads the home document and presents mission, programs, impact, needs, stories, and calls to action. No personal data is required.</p><p>Flow B is volunteer interest. The visitor submits name, email, and preferred help type. The current behavior shows a thank-you message and resets the form; it does not send the data to a server.</p><p>Flow C is donation intention. The visitor chooses type, currency, amount, identity fields, and anonymity. donation.js derives an INR amount, constructs a display amount and expenditure explanation, stores an activity record, optionally adds a public donor, and opens the payment modal.</p><p>Flow D is profile viewing. A signed-in browser user enters an email and the script filters stored activity by case-insensitive email match. The UI shows total, count, date, and a category-specific detail.</p><table><tr><th>Flow</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Storage</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Navigation</td><td>Public sections</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Volunteer fields</td><td>Success message</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Donation fields</td><td>Payment modal</td><td>Activity, donors</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Profile email</td><td>Private activity summary</td><td>Activity read</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.2 System Model: Conceptual ERD</h1><p>The current browser data is not a relational database, but a conceptual ERD helps explain how the prototype can evolve. A User represents the browser-local supporter profile. A Contribution represents a donation intention and includes amount, currency display, type, email, date, and private expenditure text.</p><p>A PublicSupporter is a projection of a contribution that the donor allowed to appear publicly. In the current implementation this is stored as a donor object with name, cause, amount, and isPublic.</p><p>A Campaign is represented visually by tracker cards and progress values. In production it should be a managed record with goals, categories, status, and verified totals rather than hardcoded HTML.</p><p>A PaymentAttempt is a future server-side record connected to a provider reference and verification status. The current payment modal is only a next-step presentation and must not be treated as a successful payment.</p><pre>USER (email, name, signedInAt)<br/>   1 -------- * CONTRIBUTION (cause, amount, date)<br/>                         |<br/>                         | optional public consent<br/>                         v<br/>                  PUBLIC_SUPPORTER<br/><br/>CAMPAIGN (title, goal, verifiedTotal)<br/>   1 -------- * CONTRIBUTION<br/><br/>CONTRIBUTION ---- 0..1 PAYMENT_ATTEMPT (providerRef, status)</pre></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.3 Data Model</h1><p>The browser data model uses JSON arrays and objects rather than a schema migration. careconnectUser stores name, normalized email, and signedInAt. careconnectDonors stores donor display objects. careconnectActivity stores email, cause, amount, inrAmount, expenditure, and date.</p><p>The amount model retains an INR base amount and renders the selected currency using a fixed client-side rate table. This is useful for a visual demo but cannot support financial settlement because rates become stale and are not authoritative.</p><p>The data model separates public display from private activity through the anonymous flag. The donor is added to the public array only when anonymity is not selected. The activity is still stored for the profile in both cases.</p><p>A production schema should use UUID identifiers, foreign keys, numeric money types, UTC timestamps, immutable payment records, reversals instead of destructive edits, and a consent field with an auditable timestamp.</p><table><tr><th>Key</th><th>Fields</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>careconnectUser</td><td>name, email, signedInAt</td><td>Demo profile state</td></tr><tr><td>careconnectDonors</td><td>name, cause, amount, isPublic</td><td>Public supporter projection</td></tr><tr><td>careconnectActivity</td><td>email, cause, amount, inrAmount, expenditure, date</td><td>Private browser activity</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.3 Data Lifecycle and Privacy</h1><p>Data enters the prototype through name and email fields in the donation and login forms. HTML required and email inputs provide basic syntactic validation. JavaScript trims the email and lowercases it during login; the donation workflow trims the donation email.</p><p>The activity record is created before the payment modal opens. This ordering makes the demo profile immediately useful but would be unsafe for a real system because an intention is not a confirmed payment.</p><p>Public donor display is derived from the donor array and limited to entries with isPublic not equal to false. The preview renders the first five visible supporters, while the full modal renders the complete visible list.</p><p>Users can clear localStorage through browser controls, but the current interface does not provide a formal export, deletion, or retention policy. Production work must add these controls and document them in plain language.</p><div class='lifecycle'>Collect minimal fields â†’ validate â†’ store browser-local demo record â†’ render private/public projection â†’ user may clear browser data</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.4 User Interface Design</h1><p>The interface uses a consistent visual vocabulary across pages. DM Sans supports compact labels and body text, while Playfair Display creates a human and editorial tone for major headings. Green communicates care and trust; lime marks actions and progress.</p><p>The home page is organized into a hero, impact strip, story, programs, needs, tracker, quote, volunteer form, footer, donation modal, and chat widget. Anchors allow the user to move within the long page without route changes.</p><p>The donation page uses a two-column arrangement for contribution form and supporters, followed by a private profile section. Modals keep payment details and the full supporter list available without leaving the page.</p><p>The login page is intentionally narrow and focused. It explains the private profile purpose, collects demo credentials, and repeats the warning not to use a real password.</p><pre>Header / navigation<br/>        â†“<br/>Hero â†’ Impact â†’ Story â†’ Programs<br/>        â†“<br/>Needs â†’ Tracker â†’ Story quote<br/>        â†“<br/>Volunteer form â†’ Footer<br/>        â†˜ shared helper chat</pre></section>
<section class='page'><h1>4.4 Responsive and Accessible UI</h1><p>Responsive behavior is supported by flexible grids, max-width shells, viewport-aware typography, and a mobile menu toggle. The CSS keeps the page centered within a readable maximum width while allowing content to breathe on large screens.</p><p>Interactive elements use buttons for actions and anchors for navigation. Forms use labels, required attributes, email types, autocomplete hints, aria-live message regions, and dialog labels.</p><p>Modals have close buttons and can be closed by clicking the overlay or pressing Escape. The chat toggle exposes aria-expanded and aria-controls. The mobile menu similarly updates aria-expanded.</p><p>Accessibility should be validated beyond source inspection. Future testing should cover keyboard tab order, focus return after modal close, contrast ratios, screen-reader announcements, reduced motion preferences, zoom to 200 percent, and touch target size.</p><table><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Implemented treatment</th><th>Further check</th></tr><tr><td>Form labeling</td><td>Labels and placeholders</td><td>Screen reader audit</td></tr><tr><td>Feedback</td><td>aria-live messages</td><td>Announcement timing</td></tr><tr><td>Dialog control</td><td>Close, overlay, Escape</td><td>Focus trap and return</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile navigation</td><td>Toggle and state attribute</td><td>Small-device test</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5 Implementation Details</h1><p>The implementation is deliberately dependency-light. There is no package manifest or build pipeline; the pages reference browser JavaScript directly and load fonts from Google Fonts.</p><p>HTML files contain the content and the hooks used by JavaScript. The scripts rely on document.querySelector, event listeners, template strings, localStorage, JSON serialization, and native form validity.</p><p>CSS is centralized in styles.css. This creates a consistent design system but also means that a selector change can affect several pages. The shared stylesheet should therefore be regression-tested whenever a component class changes.</p><p>The repository is deployable as a static artifact. The GitHub Actions workflow checks out the repository, configures Pages, uploads the root directory, and deploys it to the GitHub Pages environment.</p><table><tr><th>Technology</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>HTML5</td><td>Semantic page structure and forms</td></tr><tr><td>CSS3</td><td>Design system, layout, responsive rules</td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Client interaction and persistence</td></tr><tr><td>Web Storage API</td><td>Browser-local demo state</td></tr><tr><td>GitHub Actions</td><td>Static deployment</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.1 File-Level Implementation Map</h1><p>index.html defines the public information architecture and includes the home-page sections, donation modal, volunteer form, tracker tabs, footer, and chat widget. It references styles.css, chatbot.js, and script.js.</p><p>donation.html defines the giving experience, profile panel, payment modal, full supporter list modal, footer, and chat widget. It references styles.css, chatbot.js, and donation.js.</p><p>login.html defines the demo login form and shared chat widget. auth.js validates the form and writes careconnectUser before redirecting to donation.html#personal-profile.</p><p>script.js and donation.js both use DOM ids and class names as integration points. chatbot.js is shared and uses a small keyword table. styles.css provides visual behavior for the selectors used by every page.</p><table><tr><th>File</th><th>Key responsibilities</th></tr><tr><td>index.html</td><td>Public home content and controls</td></tr><tr><td>donation.html</td><td>Giving, profile, payment, supporters</td></tr><tr><td>login.html</td><td>Demo login entry point</td></tr><tr><td>script.js</td><td>Home interactions</td></tr><tr><td>donation.js</td><td>Giving and activity interactions</td></tr><tr><td>auth.js</td><td>Login validation and redirect</td></tr><tr><td>chatbot.js</td><td>Keyword helper</td></tr><tr><td>styles.css</td><td>Shared presentation</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.2 Home Page Implementation</h1><p>The home page starts with an announcement bar and sticky header. Navigation anchors point to section ids, while Donate points to the dedicated donation page and Log in points to the login page.</p><p>The hero section combines the central statement, supporting copy, two calls to action, a community avatar stack, and a visual image card. The impact strip displays three headline measures: children supported, learning communities, and hours of learning.</p><p>The program cards communicate learning, nutrition and wellbeing, and mentorship. The needs section translates contribution amounts into concrete outcomes. The tracker section uses data-category attributes so script.js can show all campaigns or one category at a time.</p><p>The volunteer form is intentionally lightweight. A valid submit prevents navigation, writes a thank-you message into the form message region, and resets the form. It is a lead-capture demonstration, not a backend application.</p><div class='implementation-note'>Home page behavior: navigation toggle â†’ tracker filtering â†’ volunteer acknowledgement â†’ donation modal â†’ shared chat.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.3 Donation Page Implementation</h1><p>The donation page supports four contribution types. Clicking a type changes the selected class. Preset amount buttons use data-inr values and clear a custom amount. Currency selection updates the visible preset labels and the custom input placeholder.</p><p>When the form submits, donation.js determines whether a custom amount is present. It converts the chosen amount to an INR base using the fixed currencyRates object, formats a display amount using currencySymbols, and identifies the selected cause.</p><p>For a money contribution, the script generates a demonstration expenditure statement with 70 percent education materials, 20 percent meals, and 10 percent centre care. The values are explanatory demo text, not an audited allocation.</p><p>The script creates random demo account, branch, and UPI values, sets payment modal fields, requests a QR image, writes activity to localStorage, conditionally adds a public donor, renders lists, and opens the payment modal.</p><div class='implementation-note'>Donation page behavior: select â†’ convert â†’ record demo activity â†’ optionally publish donor â†’ show next-step modal.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.4 Authentication and Profile Implementation</h1><p>The authentication surface is a demo identity flow. auth.js attaches a submit listener to loginForm, prevents the default navigation, checks native form validity, verifies the six-character minimum, and writes a JSON user object to localStorage.</p><p>The password is not written to localStorage. The stored object contains name, email, and signedInAt. This avoids persisting the demo password but does not make the flow secure or authenticated.</p><p>On the donation page, setupProfile reads careconnectUser. If present, it fills the profile fields, changes the account navigation to Log out, renders activity, and adds a listener that removes the user key and reloads the page.</p><p>If no user is present, the profile button becomes a route to login.html and the activity button becomes a route to the login page. Activity filtering is case-insensitive by email.</p><table><tr><th>State</th><th>UI behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Guest</td><td>Guest profile; log in actions</td></tr><tr><td>Signed in</td><td>Name, email, avatar initials, activity</td></tr><tr><td>Logout</td><td>Remove user key and reload</td></tr><tr><td>No activity</td><td>Private message, hidden activity panel</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.5 Chatbot Implementation</h1><p>The helper chatbot is a small deterministic FAQ component. It opens and closes through the chat toggle, updates aria-expanded, focuses the input when opened, and appends user and bot messages to the chat message list.</p><p>The getAnswer function lowercases the question, checks a privacy branch first, then searches a list of keyword groups. It can answer about donations, currencies, anonymous giving, profiles, volunteering, and greetings.</p><p>Unknown questions receive a safe fallback that names the topics the helper understands. The component does not call an AI service or send messages to a server.</p><p>The design is appropriate for common static answers, but a production help system should provide managed content, escalation to human support, analytics with consent, abuse controls, and localization.</p><table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Example trigger</th><th>Response purpose</th></tr><tr><td>Donation</td><td>donate, give</td><td>Explain supported contribution types</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy</td><td>anonymous, private</td><td>Explain public-list choice</td></tr><tr><td>Profile</td><td>profile, history</td><td>Explain login and activity</td></tr><tr><td>Volunteer</td><td>volunteer, help</td><td>Suggest participation</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.6 Styling and Design System</h1><p>The stylesheet starts with design tokens for ink, muted text, cream, paper, green, lime, yellow, mint, and line colors. This makes the visual language easy to adjust and keeps the pages coherent.</p><p>Shared components include announcement bars, site headers, brands, buttons, text links, section shells, impact statistics, program cards, need rows, modals, forms, donor rows, profile cards, and chat widgets.</p><p>The stylesheet uses CSS Grid for major page regions, Flexbox for aligned control groups, max-width containers for readable content, border radii for warmth, and shadows for elevated notes and dialogs.</p><p>The external Unsplash image URL and Google Fonts links create runtime dependencies. A production deployment should consider self-hosting approved assets, providing fallbacks, adding content security policy, and checking license and availability obligations.</p><div class='color-swatch'><span class='green'>Green trust</span><span class='lime'>Lime action</span><span class='cream'>Cream paper</span><span class='mint'>Mint support</span></div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.1 Software / Hardware Specifications</h1><p>The prototype requires only a modern web browser with JavaScript enabled, CSS support, localStorage support, and network access for remote fonts, images, and the QR image request. It can be served by any static file host.</p><p>For development, a code editor such as Visual Studio Code, Git, and a simple static server are sufficient. Opening files directly may work in a browser, but serving through HTTP more closely matches a deployed environment.</p><p>Recommended client hardware is any current laptop, desktop, tablet, or smartphone capable of running a modern browser. The design should be tested at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths.</p><p>For the proposed production platform, the README recommends React, Java Spring Boot, PostgreSQL, Spring Security, Bean Validation, Flyway, Actuator, Maven, Docker Compose, and GitHub Actions. Those technologies are not present in the current repository and must not be described as implemented.</p><table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Current prototype</th><th>Recommended production</th></tr><tr><td>Frontend</td><td>HTML, CSS, browser JS</td><td>React and accessible component system</td></tr><tr><td>Backend</td><td>None</td><td>Spring Boot REST API</td></tr><tr><td>Database</td><td>localStorage</td><td>PostgreSQL and Flyway</td></tr><tr><td>Hosting</td><td>GitHub Pages</td><td>HTTPS app and managed database</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>5.8 Deployment and Operations</h1><p>The GitHub Pages workflow triggers on pushes to main or manual dispatch. It grants contents read, Pages write, and ID-token write permissions, checks out the repository, configures Pages, uploads the repository root, and deploys the artifact.</p><p>This deployment is appropriate for the static prototype because all runtime behavior is delivered to the browser. It does not provide secrets storage, server logs, payment webhooks, database backups, or private routes.</p><p>Before a production launch, the public domain should use HTTPS, security headers, a controlled content policy, a privacy notice, monitored error reporting, dependency updates, and a release process that runs tests before deployment.</p><p>The deployment workflow should eventually build and test the frontend, build and test the backend, scan dependencies, validate database migrations, publish versioned artifacts, and require review for changes to financial or child-safety functionality.</p><div class='deployment-pipeline'>Commit â†’ CI checks â†’ review â†’ static artifact / application image â†’ deploy â†’ smoke test â†’ monitor</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6 System Outputs and Validation</h1><p>System outputs are the visible results of the project: rendered pages, progress cards, validation messages, donor rows, profile summaries, payment instructions, modal states, chatbot replies, and deployment artifacts.</p><p>Validation is considered at three levels. HTML and browser constraints validate basic input shape. JavaScript validates workflow conditions and updates messages. Manual and automated tests should validate that the complete journey remains coherent.</p><p>Because the project has no formal test suite, this report defines a repeatable validation matrix based on the source behavior. The matrix can be converted into browser end-to-end tests when a test runner is introduced.</p><p>The output review must also check truthful labeling: a successful local write is not a successful payment; a demo login is not authentication; and a static campaign number is not a verified financial report.</p><div class='output-rule'>Every output should answer: What happened? What data was used? Is it private, public, demo, or verified?</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.1 Output Screen: Home</h1><p>The home output begins with an announcement bar and a sticky header. The brand is shown as careconnect with a heart mark. The navigation links provide both in-page discovery and route changes to donation and login.</p><p>The hero output presents the promise Every child deserves a chance to shine, a supporting description, a Make a difference button, an Explore our work link, and a small social-proof treatment.</p><p>The impact output is a dark green band with three headline values. These values are visible communication content and should be connected to verified aggregate records in a production build.</p><p>The lower output continues with story, program cards, needs, donation tracker, a quote, volunteer form, footer, modal, and chat. The page therefore supports both emotional context and practical action.</p><div class='screen-description'><strong>Expected visible output:</strong> A polished long-form NGO landing page with an empathetic visual hierarchy and clear action buttons.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.1 Output Screen: Programs and Needs</h1><p>The programs section presents three cards: Learning together, Growing stronger, and Dreaming bigger. Each card uses a distinct background treatment while sharing the same structure of number, icon, title, explanation, and action arrow.</p><p>The needs section presents three concrete contribution examples: a school supplies kit at &#226;‚&#185;500, a warm healthy meal at &#226;‚&#185;250, and learning support at &#226;‚&#185;1,000 per month.</p><p>This output format helps a visitor map an amount to a purpose without forcing the visitor to understand internal accounting. It is an effective communication pattern, but each claim must be backed by an internal allocation policy if used in production.</p><p>The volunteer section complements donation actions by offering time, skills, or fundraising as alternative forms of participation. The form&#226;€™s success output is deliberately short and positive.</p><table><tr><th>Card</th><th>Output message</th><th>Interaction</th></tr><tr><td>Learning</td><td>After-school curiosity</td><td>Anchor to involvement</td></tr><tr><td>Growing</td><td>Nutrition and wellbeing</td><td>Anchor to involvement</td></tr><tr><td>Dreaming</td><td>Mentorship and skills</td><td>Anchor to involvement</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.1 Output Screen: Donation</h1><p>The donation output provides a clear heading, contribution type cards, currency selector, preset amounts, custom amount input, name and email fields, anonymity checkbox, privacy note, secure continuation button, and a demo-payment warning.</p><p>The supporter panel presents a preview of visible donors, a count, a full-list action, and a privacy statement. The list is rendered from initial HTML data or browser-stored donors.</p><p>The private profile output shows a guest or logged-in state. For a matching email with activity, the page displays the total, contribution count, cause, date, and a private-use detail for money contributions.</p><p>The payment output is a modal containing demo account name, bank, account number, IFSC, UPI ID, a QR image, an email notice, and a Done button. It is a next-step instruction screen, not a settlement confirmation.</p><div class='screen-description'><strong>Expected visible output:</strong> A two-column giving experience that is transparent about demo status and separates public supporters from private activity.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.1 Output Screen: Login and Chat</h1><p>The login output is intentionally focused. It presents a private-profile explanation and a form with name, email, and password. The submit button says Log in, while the small note warns users not to use a real password.</p><p>If native validation fails, the browser displays its validation UI and the script returns without writing storage. If the password is shorter than six characters, the page writes a specific message.</p><p>A valid submission stores the demo user and redirects to the donation profile section. The user then sees their name, email, avatar initials, and activity if an activity email matches.</p><p>The chat output is shared across pages. It opens as an overlay panel, shows an initial greeting, appends the user question, and gives a deterministic answer or fallback.</p><table><tr><th>State</th><th>Expected message</th></tr><tr><td>Invalid required field</td><td>Browser-native validity prompt</td></tr><tr><td>Password under six</td><td>Please use at least 6 characters for the demo password.</td></tr><tr><td>Successful login</td><td>Redirect to private profile</td></tr><tr><td>Unknown chat topic</td><td>Supported-topic fallback response</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.2 Input Validation</h1><p>The forms use native HTML validation as the first line of defense. required attributes prevent empty values, type=email checks basic email syntax, type=number limits custom amounts to a minimum of 1 and step 0.01, and minlength=6 applies to the demo password.</p><p>JavaScript performs additional checks before workflow updates. auth.js calls checkValidity and reportValidity. donation.js calls the profile email validity methods when the user requests activity. script.js validates the homepage modal email before acknowledging a donation intention.</p><p>The donation amount logic uses Number and treats a non-zero custom amount as the selected amount. The UI should be tested for negative, zero, decimal, very large, and blank custom values. The native min rule covers some cases, but production validation must be repeated on a server.</p><p>Validation messages should be concise, nearby, and honest about what the user must correct. They should never imply that a payment succeeded when only local demo activity was written.</p><table><tr><th>Input</th><th>Rule</th><th>Expected result</th></tr><tr><td>Name</td><td>Required text</td><td>Submit blocked if empty</td></tr><tr><td>Email</td><td>Required email format</td><td>Validity prompt</td></tr><tr><td>Password</td><td>Minimum six characters</td><td>Specific demo message</td></tr><tr><td>Custom amount</td><td>Minimum 1, step 0.01</td><td>Invalid amount blocked</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.2 Privacy and Consent Validation</h1><p>Anonymous giving is a visible checkbox rather than an implicit assumption. When selected, the contribution is written to the private activity array but is not unshifted into the public donor array.</p><p>The public list renderer filters out donors whose isPublic value is false. Existing static donor rows are treated as public when first read from the page. A production implementation must store consent as a deliberate, auditable record rather than infer it from a display object.</p><p>Profile lookup is constrained in the demo by the signed-in state and a matching email filter. This is not authorization because a user can manipulate localStorage. A real system must enforce ownership at the API boundary.</p><p>The content also uses privacy messaging as a validation layer: users are told where activity is stored, that public names require permission, and that real passwords should not be used.</p><div class='privacy-checklist'><strong>Privacy checks:</strong><br/>[x] Anonymous option visible<br/>[x] Public list excludes anonymous additions<br/>[x] Demo storage disclosed<br/>[x] No child records in prototype<br/>[ ] Server-side ownership enforcement (future)<br/>[ ] Consent audit trail (future)</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.3 Report Format</h1><p>A production NGO reporting module should generate reports from verified records and preserve the distinction between operational, financial, and public-impact views.</p><p>A monthly public impact report may include programs active, sessions delivered, aggregate attendance rate, supplies distributed, volunteer hours, donation totals, expenses by category, and a reconciliation status. It must exclude names, child identifiers, screening notes, payment credentials, and private donor details.</p><p>An internal financial report should include immutable transaction identifiers, dates, source references, category, amount, approval status, and reconciliation evidence. Confirmed transactions should be corrected through reversals or adjustments rather than deletion.</p><p>The current prototype&#226;€™s campaign cards are a visual report format only. They are useful for demonstrating hierarchy and progress but do not constitute a financial report.</p><table><tr><th>Report type</th><th>Audience</th><th>Safe content</th></tr><tr><td>Impact summary</td><td>Public</td><td>Verified aggregates and methodology</td></tr><tr><td>Donation register</td><td>Authorized finance staff</td><td>Provider references and status</td></tr><tr><td>Program report</td><td>Staff and funders</td><td>Sessions and aggregate outcomes</td></tr><tr><td>Audit log</td><td>Authorized administrators</td><td>Actor, action, entity, timestamp</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.3 Sample Report Data Dictionary</h1><p>A report is only as reliable as its data dictionary. Each metric should have a name, definition, source, aggregation rule, time range, owner, and publication status.</p><p>Children supported should define whether it counts unique participants, enrollments, or service contacts. Learning hours should define whether it counts scheduled or attended hours. Donations should distinguish pledged, received, failed, refunded, and reconciled values.</p><p>Campaign progress should calculate from verified received contributions against a stated goal, not from a front-end constant. Food and clothes quantities should define units and treatment of in-kind valuation.</p><p>The public page should publish the date of the latest update and link to a methodology note. This improves trust without disclosing sensitive records.</p><table><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Definition question</th><th>Required metadata</th></tr><tr><td>Children supported</td><td>Unique or service contacts?</td><td>Period, program scope</td></tr><tr><td>Donation total</td><td>Received or pledged?</td><td>Currency, reconciliation</td></tr><tr><td>Volunteer hours</td><td>Scheduled or verified?</td><td>Approval and period</td></tr><tr><td>Campaign progress</td><td>Which transactions count?</td><td>Goal, status, update time</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.4 Test Matrix: Navigation and UI</h1><p>Validation should start with the paths that every visitor can use. The home page must load without console errors, the header links must navigate to the correct anchors or pages, and the mobile menu must open, close, and update its accessibility state.</p><p>The tracker tabs must select All campaigns by default, activate the clicked tab, and hide cards whose data-category does not match the selected category. Returning to All campaigns must show every card.</p><p>The home donation modal must open from Give with purpose, focus the first input, validate the email, acknowledge a valid intention, and close through the close button, overlay, or Escape.</p><p>The volunteer form must reject missing required values through the browser, show a thank-you message for valid values, and reset after submission.</p><table><tr><th>Case</th><th>Steps</th><th>Expected</th></tr><tr><td>UI-01</td><td>Open home page</td><td>All major sections render</td></tr><tr><td>UI-02</td><td>Switch tracker category</td><td>Only matching cards visible</td></tr><tr><td>UI-03</td><td>Open and close modal</td><td>State and focus behave correctly</td></tr><tr><td>UI-04</td><td>Submit volunteer form</td><td>Message shown and form reset</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.4 Test Matrix: Donation and Profile</h1><p>Donation tests should cover each contribution type, each supported currency, all preset values, a valid custom amount, invalid custom values, required identity fields, anonymous mode, and public mode.</p><p>After a valid submission, the activity array should contain a new record with the selected cause, amount, base amount, date, and expenditure text where appropriate. The payment modal should have non-empty demo detail fields and a QR source.</p><p>When anonymous mode is selected, the activity should exist but the donor list count should not increase. When public mode is selected, the donor should appear in the full list and the preview if among the first five.</p><p>Profile tests should cover guest redirection, signed-in rendering, case-insensitive email matching, no-activity messaging, total calculation, contribution count, logout, and clearing the activity panel for an unknown email.</p><table><tr><th>Case</th><th>Expected evidence</th></tr><tr><td>DN-01 Valid public donation</td><td>Activity and donor records added</td></tr><tr><td>DN-02 Valid anonymous donation</td><td>Activity added; public list unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>DN-03 Currency change</td><td>Preset labels and placeholder update</td></tr><tr><td>PR-01 Matching profile</td><td>Total and activity list visible</td></tr><tr><td>PR-02 Unknown email</td><td>No-donations message visible</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.4 Test Matrix: Login and Chat</h1><p>Login tests should verify that empty name, invalid email, empty password, and short password do not create a user. A valid six-character password should create only the intended user fields and redirect to the profile anchor.</p><p>Logout should remove the careconnectUser key and restore the guest state after reload. The application should not store the password in the user object.</p><p>Chat tests should verify opening and closing, focus behavior, empty submission handling, keyword matching, privacy precedence, message rendering as text, and a safe response for unknown topics.</p><p>Because the chatbot uses textContent when adding messages, the test should confirm that user input is not interpreted as HTML. This is a useful client-side safety property.</p><table><tr><th>Case</th><th>Input</th><th>Expected</th></tr><tr><td>AU-01</td><td>Password abc</td><td>Minimum-length message</td></tr><tr><td>AU-02</td><td>Valid demo credentials</td><td>User key and redirect</td></tr><tr><td>AU-03</td><td>Click Log out</td><td>User key removed</td></tr><tr><td>CH-01</td><td>Can I give anonymously?</td><td>Privacy response</td></tr><tr><td>CH-02</td><td>HTML-like input</td><td>Rendered as text</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.5 Validation Results and Evidence</h1><p>Source inspection confirms that the implementation contains the expected page and script boundaries, native form constraints, localStorage keys, modals, tracker categories, and deployment workflow.</p><p>The code visibly prevents default form navigation for volunteer, login, donation, and chat submissions. It also uses textContent for user-provided donor and chat values in rendered rows, reducing the risk of treating those values as markup.</p><p>The report treats source inspection as evidence of intended behavior, not as a substitute for browser execution. A complete acceptance cycle should run the matrix in a real browser at multiple viewport widths and with a clean storage profile.</p><p>No claim is made that the QR request succeeds, that a payment is received, or that a user is securely authenticated. Those outcomes require an external service and server-side verification.</p><div class='evidence-box'><strong>Evidence basis:</strong> repository source review, implementation map, explicit demo notices, and a defined manual/end-to-end validation matrix. Recommended next step: automate the matrix with Playwright.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>6.6 Implemented Versus Proposed Capability</h1><p>Accurate project reporting depends on distinguishing delivered behavior from roadmap intent. The current repository implements the public portal and browser-side demo interactions, while the README describes a broader target system.</p><p>Implemented capabilities include static pages, responsive styling, donation categories, currency display, local activity, public/anonymous donor handling, a demo login, helper chatbot, modal workflows, and GitHub Pages deployment.</p><p>Proposed capabilities include staff accounts, roles, secure sessions, programs, sessions, attendance, inventory transactions, campaigns, real donation records, expenses, volunteer approval, dashboards, audit logs, database migrations, and payment gateway webhooks.</p><p>This distinction should be kept in the final submission, presentation, and README so reviewers can understand both the current achievement and the engineering roadmap.</p><table><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Evidence or next step</th></tr><tr><td>Public portal</td><td>Implemented</td><td>index.html</td></tr><tr><td>Demo donation flow</td><td>Implemented</td><td>donation.html and donation.js</td></tr><tr><td>Secure authentication</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>Backend and Spring Security</td></tr><tr><td>PostgreSQL data</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>Schema and migrations</td></tr><tr><td>Staff operations</td><td>Roadmap</td><td>Protected portal modules</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>7 Conclusion</h1><p>CareConnect demonstrates a coherent public NGO experience with a strong visual identity, clear calls to action, multiple contribution paths, impact communication, volunteer engagement, a browser-local supporter profile, and a small helper chatbot.</p><p>The strongest implementation decision is the explicit separation of public communication, private activity, and demo payment instructions. The system gives users useful feedback while warning them about the limits of the prototype.</p><p>The strongest architectural opportunity is the seam between the friendly public experience and a secure operational backend. The existing content and interaction model can become the public layer of a larger platform without exposing children&#226;€™s records.</p><p>The project is successful as a static prototype and incomplete as a production donation system. Its next phase should prioritize security, trusted data, payment verification, test automation, and staff workflows rather than adding more decorative content.</p><div class='conclusion-box'>Conclusion: a credible front-end foundation is present; production readiness requires a server-authoritative data and security layer.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>7 Recommendations</h1><p>First, preserve the current public experience while introducing a small backend for supporter records and payment intent tracking. Keep the public API limited to published content and aggregate metrics.</p><p>Second, implement hosted payment processing in test mode. Record payment intents, verify signed webhooks, reconcile provider statuses, and show a success state only after trusted confirmation.</p><p>Third, add an internal staff portal with roles for administrators, educators, volunteer coordinators, and finance staff. Enforce permissions in services and controllers, not only in the user interface.</p><p>Fourth, build auditability into inventory and finance. Stock changes should update quantity and transaction history atomically. Confirmed financial records should be adjusted or reversed rather than deleted.</p><p>Fifth, add automated unit, integration, authorization, privacy, and end-to-end tests before expanding feature scope.</p><ol><li>Backend and data authority</li><li>Verified payment integration</li><li>Role-based staff portal</li><li>Audit and reconciliation</li><li>Automated quality gates</li></ol></section>
<section class='page'><h1>7 Limitations</h1><p>The most important limitation is the absence of a server. Browser-local values can be changed, deleted, copied, or inspected by the user. They cannot establish identity, authorization, financial truth, or organizational continuity.</p><p>The demo login is not authentication. It does not verify credentials, hash a password, expire a session, handle recovery, or defend against brute force.</p><p>The payment experience is not a payment integration. Random demo details and an externally requested QR image do not confirm receipt, identity, amount, or destination.</p><p>Impact figures, donor examples, and campaign progress are static or synthetic. They should not be presented as audited organizational reporting.</p><p>The project does not yet include a content management workflow, localization, formal accessibility audit, consent management, analytics governance, backend monitoring, backups, or incident response.</p><div class='warning'>Limitations must be shown to reviewers and users. Hiding them would create a trust and safety problem.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>7 Future Scope</h1><p>The future scope follows the roadmap in the project README. A React frontend can organize public and protected routes, while a modular Spring Boot monolith can provide controllers, services, repositories, DTO validation, security, and audit logging.</p><p>PostgreSQL can store users, programs, sessions, attendance records, inventory items, stock transactions, campaigns, donations, expenses, volunteers, assignments, and audit logs with UUID keys, timestamps, constraints, and foreign keys.</p><p>A public impact dashboard can compute aggregate metrics from trusted records. A staff dashboard can combine donation totals, expenses, low-stock alerts, upcoming classes, attendance, and volunteer activity.</p><p>Privacy and safeguarding should remain central. Use synthetic data for portfolio demonstrations, pseudonymous student references, restricted APIs, consented stories, data retention rules, and legal review before any real child data is processed.</p><div class='roadmap'>Prototype â†’ secure API â†’ operational modules â†’ verified reporting â†’ monitored production service</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>7 Future Scope Roadmap</h1><p>Phase 1 should stabilize the prototype: remove brittle assumptions, add a static build check, create browser smoke tests, improve focus handling, and document the demo data policy.</p><p>Phase 2 should create the backend foundation: project structure, database migrations, user roles, secure sessions, DTO validation, error responses, and a minimal public content API.</p><p>Phase 3 should implement core operations: education programs, sessions, attendance aggregates, inventory transactions, volunteer applications, campaigns, and donation intents.</p><p>Phase 4 should add finance and reporting: expenses, reconciliation, audit logs, aggregate dashboards, exports, provider webhooks, and monthly report generation.</p><p>Phase 5 should harden and deploy: threat modeling, dependency scanning, backups, monitoring, incident response, accessibility review, privacy review, and a controlled production release.</p><table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Deliverable</th><th>Success signal</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Stable demo</td><td>Automated smoke tests pass</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Secure foundation</td><td>Protected API and migrations</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Operations</td><td>Staff workflows use trusted data</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Reporting</td><td>Reconciled and auditable reports</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Production readiness</td><td>Security and accessibility sign-off</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>8 Bibliography and References</h1><p>CareConnectNGO repository source files: index.html, donation.html, login.html, styles.css, script.js, donation.js, auth.js, chatbot.js, README.md, and .github/workflows/pages.yml. Accessed 27 October 2026.</p><p>World Wide Web Consortium. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2. Reference for perceivable, operable, understandable, and robust web experiences.</p><p>OWASP Foundation. Application Security Verification Standard and OWASP Top 10. Reference for authentication, access control, input handling, injection prevention, and secure design.</p><p>OWASP Foundation. User Privacy Protection Cheat Sheet. Reference for data minimization, purpose limitation, consent, retention, and user control.</p><p>PCI Security Standards Council. Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard. Reference for avoiding storage of cardholder data and using secure payment processing.</p><p>Mozilla Developer Network. Web Storage API, HTML forms, Constraint Validation API, ARIA, dialog patterns, and Fetch-related browser documentation.</p><p>GitHub Docs. GitHub Pages and GitHub Actions deployment documentation. Reference for the static deployment workflow used by this repository.</p><p>For a real Indian NGO deployment, obtain current legal and safeguarding advice on child protection, consent, charitable operations, and applicable data protection requirements before processing personal data.</p><div class='reference-note'>References are guidance sources. They do not convert the current browser demo into a compliant payment, identity, or child-data system.</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Appendix A: User Guide</h1><p>To explore the public portal, open index.html through a static server or deployed GitHub Pages site. Use the header links to move among sections. Try the tracker tabs to filter campaign categories.</p><p>Use Give with purpose to open the homepage donation-intention modal. Provide a syntactically valid email and choose an amount to see the acknowledgement. This path is separate from the full donation page.</p><p>On the Donate page, choose a contribution type, currency, preset or custom amount, name, and email. Select anonymous giving if the name should not be added to the visible supporter list. Continue securely opens demo payment details.</p><p>On the Log in page, use a synthetic name, valid email, and a password of at least six characters. The session is browser-local. Never enter a real password or real payment information.</p><div class='user-guide'>Recommended demo data: Name: Demo Supporter; Email: supporter@example.com; Password: example6</div></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Appendix B: Traceability Matrix</h1><p>Traceability connects requested report topics to repository evidence. The matrix helps a reviewer verify that the report is grounded in implementation rather than generated independently of the project.</p><p>The public portal requirement traces to index.html. Donation behavior traces to donation.html and donation.js. Login behavior traces to login.html and auth.js. Chat behavior traces to chatbot.js. Presentation and responsive behavior trace to styles.css.</p><p>The deployment requirement traces to pages.yml. The production architecture and schema recommendations trace to README.md and are explicitly marked as proposed.</p><p>This traceability approach should be retained when future modules are added so requirements, code, tests, and reports remain aligned.</p><table><tr><th>Report topic</th><th>Repository evidence</th><th>Status</th></tr><tr><td>Public portal</td><td>index.html</td><td>Implemented</td></tr><tr><td>Giving workflow</td><td>donation.html, donation.js</td><td>Implemented as demo</td></tr><tr><td>Login/profile</td><td>login.html, auth.js</td><td>Implemented as demo</td></tr><tr><td>Chat</td><td>chatbot.js</td><td>Implemented</td></tr><tr><td>Visual system</td><td>styles.css</td><td>Implemented</td></tr><tr><td>Production roadmap</td><td>README.md</td><td>Proposed</td></tr></table></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Appendix C: Suggested Production Data Tables</h1><p>The README proposes a relational model that avoids unnecessary child information and supports operational workflows. Each table should have a UUID primary key, created and updated timestamps where appropriate, constraints, and access rules.</p><p>Users represent staff accounts and roles. Programs represent education initiatives. Sessions belong to programs, and attendance records use pseudonymous references. Inventory items hold current quantity and reorder level, while stock transactions record signed movements and reasons.</p><p>Campaigns represent public fundraising goals. Donations belong to campaigns and include amount, status, and provider reference. Expenses record category, amount, date, and creator. Volunteers and assignments support applications and scheduling.</p><p>Audit logs capture sensitive administrative actions. Public APIs should expose published program details and verified aggregates only, never raw attendance, screening, or private financial details.</p><pre>users â†’ audit_logs<br/>programs â†’ sessions â†’ attendance_records<br/>inventory_items â†’ stock_transactions<br/>campaigns â†’ donations<br/>volunteers â†’ volunteer_assignments<br/>expenses â†’ users</pre></section>
<section class='page'><h1>Appendix D: Final Submission Checklist</h1><p>Before submission, confirm that the report file opens in Microsoft Word or a compatible editor and that the table of contents page numbers match the page-break plan.</p><p>Confirm that the project opens through a static server, all three pages load, the shared styles are available, and no required local assets are missing.</p><p>Run the manual validation matrix with a clean browser storage profile. Capture screenshots of the home page, donation form, payment modal, login page, profile activity, tracker filtering, and chatbot.</p><p>In the presentation, describe the prototype honestly: implemented front-end and browser-local demo behavior, with a documented path to a secure backend and verified payment integration.</p><p>Keep synthetic data in the repository. Do not commit credentials, secrets, real donor information, real child records, or payment details.</p><div class='final-checklist'>[ ] Report opens<br/>[ ] 50+ pages confirmed<br/>[ ] Source/demo boundary explained<br/>[ ] Screenshots captured<br/>[ ] Validation matrix executed<br/>[ ] No secrets or real personal data committed</div></section></body></html>